This chapter explains how to use the Basic Zone modes on the Mode Dial for quick and easy shooting. In each mode, the AF mode, drive mode, etc., are set automatically to suit the subject. In these modes, all you do is point and shoot. In addition, to help prevent mistakes caused by operating the camera improperly,  buttons and are disabled in these modes. So you need not worry about accidental errors.

<PIC:Manual10_55>
{{image description: Camera mode dial diagram with a large arrow labeled Basic zone. The dial shows icons and letters including A-DEP, M, Av, Tv, P, and automatic/basic-zone symbols.}}

Automatically, the AF mode, drive mode, and metering mode will be set for full-auto shooting.

<PIC:Manual10_56>
{{image description: Close-up of camera mode dial showing the fully automatic rectangle icon aligned with the index mark.}}

- Look in the viewfinder and press the shutter button halfway to check that the focus confirmation light is on and the exposure setting is displayed.

<PIC:Manual10_65>
{{image description: Camera operation diagram with numbered steps. The top view shows a finger pressing the shutter button halfway near the lens, and the lower view shows an LCD panel with ISO 100 and an autofocus or focus confirmation area.}}

- Look through the viewfinder and press the shutter button completely. The beeper will sound, the self-timer lamp will blink, and the shot will be taken about 10 sec. later. During the first 8 sec., the beeper beeps slowly and the lamp blinks slowly. Then during the final 2 sec., the beeper beeps faster and the lamp stays lit. During the self-timer operation, the LCD panel counts down the seconds until the picture is taken.

<PIC:Manual10_66>
{{image description: Camera top-view diagram showing a finger pressing the shutter button fully, with a flash or indicator mark near the hot shoe and step number 4.}}

